PART 1: JAVA STRING FUNDAMENTALS
===============================================================================

String in java are Immutable by default so we use StringBuffer and StringBuilder for Immutablity.


1. STRING vs char[] vs StringBuilder vs StringBuffer
----------------------------------------------------

┌─────────────────┬─────────────┬─────────────┬──────────────┬─────────────────┐
│     Type        │  Mutability │ Thread-Safe │ Performance  │    Use Case     │
├─────────────────┼─────────────┼─────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────────┤
│ String          │ Immutable   │    Yes      │    Slow      │ Few operations  │
│ char[]          │  Mutable    │     No      │    Fast      │ Character ops   │
│ StringBuilder   │  Mutable    │     No      │    Fast      │ Single thread   │
│ StringBuffer    │  Mutable    │    Yes      │   Medium     │ Multi-thread    │
└─────────────────┴─────────────┴─────────────┴──────────────┴─────────────────┘

Example:
String str = "Hello";           // Immutable
char[] arr = {'H','e','l','l','o'}; // Mutable array
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Hello"); // Mutable, not thread-safe
StringBuffer sbf = new StringBuffer("Hello");  // Mutable, thread-safe

2. STRING IMMUTABILITY & MEMORY (STRING POOL)
----------------------------------------------

💡 KEY CONCEPT: Strings are IMMUTABLE in Java!

String Pool (Heap Memory):
- Literal strings are stored in String Pool for memory optimization
- Multiple variables can reference the same string object

Memory Example:
String s1 = "Java";        // Stored in String Pool  // This is a string Variable
String s2 = "Java";        // Points to same object in pool  // this is also a String Variable
String s3 = new String("Java"); // Creates new object in heap  // String Object

Result: s1 == s2 (true), s1 == s3 (false)

3. STRING OBJECT CREATION
-------------------------

Method 1 - String Literal:
String str1 = "Hello";     // Goes to String Pool

Method 2 - new Keyword:
String str2 = new String("Hello"); // Creates new object in heap

Method 3 - Using char array:
char[] arr = {'H','e','l','l','o'};
String str3 = new String(arr);

4. equals() vs == in STRINGS
-----------------------------

== : Compares REFERENCES (memory addresses)
equals() : Compares CONTENT (actual characters)

Example:
String s1 = "Java";
String s2 = "Java";
String s3 = new String("Java");

s1 == s2        // true (same reference in pool)
s1 == s3        // false (different objects)
s1.equals(s3)   // true (same content)

🎯 GOLDEN RULE: Always use equals() for string comparison!

5. ESSENTIAL STRING METHODS
===========================

Length & Character Access:
--------------------------
length()        → Returns string length
charAt(index)   → Returns character at given index
toCharArray()   → Converts string to char array

Example:
String str = "Hello";
int len = str.length();        // 5
char ch = str.charAt(1);       // 'e'
char[] arr = str.toCharArray(); // ['H','e','l','l','o']

Substring Operations:
---------------------
substring(start)         → From start to end
substring(start, end)    → From start to end-1
indexOf(char/string)     → First occurrence index
lastIndexOf(char/string) → Last occurrence index

Example:
String str = "Java Programming";
String sub1 = str.substring(5);     // "Programming"
String sub2 = str.substring(0, 4);  // "Java"
int index1 = str.indexOf('a');      // 1
int index2 = str.lastIndexOf('a');  // 3

Search & Check Operations:
--------------------------
contains(string)     → Returns true if contains substring
startsWith(string)   → Returns true if starts with given string
endsWith(string)     → Returns true if ends with given string
isEmpty()            → Returns true if length is 0

Example:
String str = "Java Programming";
boolean contains = str.contains("Program");    // true
boolean starts = str.startsWith("Java");       // true
boolean ends = str.endsWith("ing");            // true
boolean empty = str.isEmpty();                 // false

Case & Formatting:
------------------
toUpperCase()    → Converts to uppercase
toLowerCase()    → Converts to lowercase
trim()           → Removes leading/trailing spaces

Example:
String str = "  Java Programming  ";
String upper = str.toUpperCase();     // "  JAVA PROGRAMMING  "
String lower = str.toLowerCase();     // "  java programming  "
String trimmed = str.trim();          // "Java Programming"

Split & Replace:
----------------
split(regex)              → Splits string into array
replace(old, new)         → Replaces all occurrences
replaceAll(regex, new)    → Replaces using regex
replaceFirst(regex, new)  → Replaces first occurrence

Example:
String str = "Java,Python,C++";
String[] parts = str.split(",");           // ["Java", "Python", "C++"]
String replaced = str.replace(",", "-");   // "Java-Python-C++"
String regex = "Java Programming 123";
String result = regex.replaceAll("\\d+", "XXX"); // "Java Programming XXX"

6. STRINGBUILDER & STRINGBUFFER
===============================

Why use StringBuilder/StringBuffer?
- Strings are immutable → Each operation creates new object
- For multiple operations → Use mutable alternatives
- StringBuilder: Single-threaded, faster
- StringBuffer: Multi-threaded, synchronized

Common Methods:
---------------
append(data)         → Adds data at the end
insert(index, data)  → Inserts data at given index
delete(start, end)   → Deletes characters from start to end-1
deleteCharAt(index)  → Deletes character at given index
reverse()            → Reverses the string
toString()           → Converts to String

Example:
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Hello");
sb.append(" World");           // "Hello World"
sb.insert(5, " Java");         // "Hello Java World"
sb.delete(6, 10);             // "Hello World"
sb.reverse();                 // "dlroW olleH"
String result = sb.toString(); // Convert back to String

Performance Comparison:
-----------------------
// ❌ Inefficient (creates multiple objects)
String result = "";
for(int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    result += "a";  // Each += creates new String object
}

// ✅ Efficient (single mutable object)
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for(int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    sb.append("a");  // Modifies existing object
}
String result = sb.toString();

